<h3>Part 2: Truth, Trust, and Misinformation</h3>
<div class="question">5. (Multiple Choice) Which of the following statements about online information is TRUE?</div>
<ul>
<li><span class="checkbox">&#9744;</span> If it sounds smart, it's usually correct.</li>
<li><span class="checkbox">&#9744;</span> If a lot of people repeat it, it must be true.</li>
<li><span class="checkbox">&#9744;</span> Confidence from an AI is not the same as proof.</li>
<li><span class="checkbox">&#9744;</span> If it uses big words, you can trust it without checking.</li>
</ul>
<div class="question">6. (Classification - Spot the Problem) You read an online article that says, "The moon gives off its own light." Is this Reliable, Needs Checking, or Likely False? Why?</div>
<div class="response-box">
  <p class="response-label">✍️ Student Response Area (Double-click to type)</p>
  <p class="response-placeholder">[ Type your conceptual answer or explanation here... ]</p>
</div>
<div class="response-box">
  <p class="response-label">✍️ Student Response Area (Double-click to type)</p>
  <p class="response-placeholder">[ Type your conceptual answer or explanation here... ]</p>
</div>
<div class="question">7. (Short Response) What is one way you can compare information with a "trusted source" to see if an AI's answer is correct?</div>
<div class="response-box">
  <p class="response-label">✍️ Student Response Area (Double-click to type)</p>
  <p class="response-placeholder">[ Type your conceptual answer or explanation here... ]</p>
</div>
<div class="response-box">
  <p class="response-label">✍️ Student Response Area (Double-click to type)</p>
  <p class="response-placeholder">[ Type your conceptual answer or explanation here... ]</p>
</div>
<div class="question">8. (Scenario) You ask AI for a summary of the Civil War, and it gives you a highly confident 3-sentence summary. When you read your textbook, you notice the AI left out a major battle. Which part of the Truth Filter does this relate to?</div>
<div class="response-box">
